{
 "tables": [
  {
   "id": "pron-personal-sg",
   "title": "Personal pronouns (singular)",
   "group": "pronouns",
   "subtitle": "já, ty, on, ona, ono",
   "columns": [
    "já (I)",
    "ty (you)",
    "on (he)",
    "ona (she)",
    "ono (it)"
   ],
   "rows": [
    {
     "case": "nom",
     "forms": [
      "já",
      "ty",
      "on",
      "ona",
      "ono"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "gen",
     "forms": [
      "mě / mne",
      "tebe / tě",
      "jeho / ho / něho",
      "jí / ní",
      "jeho / ho / něho"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "dat",
     "forms": [
      "mně / mi",
      "tobě / ti",
      "jemu / mu / němu",
      "jí / ní",
      "jemu / mu / němu"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "acc",
     "forms": [
      "mě / mne",
      "tebe / tě",
      "jeho / ho / něho / jej",
      "ji / ni",
      "je / ho / něj"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "loc",
     "forms": [
      "mně",
      "tobě",
      "něm",
      "ní",
      "něm"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "ins",
     "forms": [
      "mnou",
      "tebou",
      "jím / ním",
      "jí / ní",
      "jím / ním"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "Short forms (mi, ti, mu, ho, tě) are the normal unstressed choice and go in second position: Dej mi to. Vidím ho.",
    "Long forms (mně, tobě, jemu, jeho) are used for emphasis or at the start of a sentence: Tobě to nedám, jemu ano.",
    "Forms starting with n- (něho, němu, ní, něm) are used after prepositions: bez něho, k ní, o něm, s ním.",
    "ty has a vocative only in the sense of calling someone (Ty!), so it isn't listed."
   ],
   "nativeReviewed": false
  },
  {
   "id": "pron-personal-pl",
   "title": "Personal pronouns (plural)",
   "group": "pronouns",
   "subtitle": "my, vy, oni",
   "columns": [
    "my (we)",
    "vy (you)",
    "oni / ony / ona (they)"
   ],
   "rows": [
    {
     "case": "nom",
     "forms": [
      "my",
      "vy",
      "oni / ony / ona"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "gen",
     "forms": [
      "nás",
      "vás",
      "jich / nich"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "dat",
     "forms": [
      "nám",
      "vám",
      "jim / nim"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "acc",
     "forms": [
      "nás",
      "vás",
      "je / ně"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "loc",
     "forms": [
      "nás",
      "vás",
      "nich"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "ins",
     "forms": [
      "námi",
      "vámi",
      "jimi / nimi"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "vy is also the polite singular 'you' (vykání). Use it with anyone you don't know well: Máte čas? Děkuji vám."
   ],
   "nativeReviewed": false
  },
  {
   "id": "pron-reflexive",
   "title": "se / si (reflexive)",
   "group": "pronouns",
   "subtitle": "oneself: has no nominative",
   "columns": [
    "reflexive"
   ],
   "rows": [
    {
     "case": "nom",
     "forms": [
      "—"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "gen",
     "forms": [
      "sebe"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "dat",
     "forms": [
      "sobě / si"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "acc",
     "forms": [
      "sebe / se"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "loc",
     "forms": [
      "sobě"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "ins",
     "forms": [
      "sebou"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "se is the short accusative and si the short dative. This is why verbs are listed as 'mýt se' (wash oneself) or 'dát si' (give oneself = have).",
    "s sebou = with you/me/oneself: Máš s sebou deštník? (Do you have an umbrella with you?)"
   ],
   "nativeReviewed": false
  },
  {
   "id": "pron-muj",
   "title": "můj, moje (my)",
   "group": "pronouns",
   "subtitle": "possessive · same pattern: tvůj, svůj",
   "columns": [
    "masc. sg",
    "fem. sg",
    "neut. sg",
    "plural"
   ],
   "rows": [
    {
     "case": "nom",
     "forms": [
      "můj",
      "má / moje",
      "mé / moje",
      "mí / moji (m. anim.) · mé / moje (m. inan., f.) · má / moje (n.)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "gen",
     "forms": [
      "mého",
      "mé / mojí",
      "mého",
      "mých"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "dat",
     "forms": [
      "mému",
      "mé / mojí",
      "mému",
      "mým"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "acc",
     "forms": [
      "mého (anim.) / můj (inan.)",
      "mou / moji",
      "mé / moje",
      "mé / moje (n. má / moje)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "loc",
     "forms": [
      "mém",
      "mé / mojí",
      "mém",
      "mých"
     ]
    },
    {
     "case": "ins",
     "forms": [
      "mým",
      "mou / mojí",
      "mým",
      "mými"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "The longer forms (moje, mojí, moji) are standard Czech and more common in speech; the short forms (má, mé, mou) sound more formal or written.",
    "svůj is used when the owner is the subject of the sentence: Petr volá své mámě (his own mum)."
   ],
   "nativeReviewed": false
  },
